"""Explainer pillar — Factory Tow Hitch by Vehicle.
No invented testing. Every receiver status, class, tow rating and tongue weight in the chart comes from the site's stored
vehicle data (db/migrations/003_vehicles.sql, including its attrs research notes), the site's trailer hitch guides and
upgrades hubs for each vehicle, or a maker or retailer page opened on 2026-10-08: the Ford 2024 F-150 and 2024 Bronco
towing guides, Nissan's 2025 Frontier press kit, Toyota's 2024 Tacoma and 2022 Tundra releases and Kia's 2024 Telluride
specifications. A fetch of the 2024 Chevrolet Tahoe brochure returned 404, so the Tahoe row stays unconfirmed.
Where the site's notes say a figure is unconfirmed, the row says so.
"""

KIND = "explainer"
SLUG = "factory-tow-hitch-by-vehicle"
CATEGORIES = ["hitches", "bike-racks"]

TITLE = "Factory Tow Hitch by Vehicle: Which Trucks and SUVs Ship With a Receiver, by Trim"
META = ("Factory receiver status, hitch class, tow rating and tongue weight for 32 truck and SUV generations, with the "
        "source and confidence shown on every row.")

FAQ = [
 ("How do I know if my truck or SUV has a factory tow hitch?",
  "Look under the rear bumper for a square receiver tube, usually 2 in across on the vehicles in this chart and 1.25 in on some small SUVs. A factory receiver sits centered behind the bumper and often has a plastic cover in the opening. Then look for a trailer socket nearby: a flat 4-pin, a round 7-pin, or both. Read the label on the receiver for its class and rating. If you bought the vehicle used, the window sticker or the dealer invoice names the package. On most of the vehicles in the chart the receiver comes with a package, not a trim, so the trim badge on the tailgate does not settle it."),
 ("Does a Class III or Class IV receiver raise my tow rating?",
  "No. The vehicle rating in the owner's manual is the ceiling, and the lowest number in the chain wins: vehicle, receiver, ball mount, ball and trailer coupler. A Class IV receiver on a 4Runner is still a 5,000 lb setup, because that is Toyota's figure for the vehicle. The reverse also applies. The site's Wrangler guide notes that the factory Mopar receiver is a Class II rated 3,500 lb, so a 2024 or later 4-door Rubicon that Jeep rates at 5,000 lb cannot use that rating through the factory part. Read both the manual and the receiver label."),
 ("Which vehicles in the chart ship with a receiver on every trim?",
  "Only two are confirmed from the maker. Toyota lists an integrated tow-hitch receiver and wiring harness as standard on all grades of the 2010–2024 4Runner in its 2017, 2019, 2020 and 2021 releases. Ford's 2024 F-150 towing guide says a 2 in receiver rated 11,600 lb is standard on the pickup, with a reinforced 14,000 lb receiver in the Tow/Haul Package. Everything else in the chart is a package, a single trim, a dealer accessory, or not confirmed. The site could not confirm a trim list from Chevrolet, GMC, Ram or Toyota for the Silverado, Sierra, Ram 1500, Tundra, Tahoe or Sequoia."),
 ("Which vehicles never come with a factory receiver?",
  "The site could not confirm any factory receiver on the 2023–2026 Honda CR-V, the 2020–2026 Toyota Highlander, the 2020–2025 Subaru Outback, the 2019–2024 Subaru Forester or the 2020–2025 Hyundai Palisade, whose Hyundai spec sheets list no tow hitch as equipment. Each of those makers sells a hitch as an accessory. On the 2019–2025 RAV4, most versions have no receiver; a Toyota dealer says the 2024 Adventure and TRD Off-Road include one with Tow Prep. Tesla's Model 3 and Model Y get a receiver only with the Tesla Tow Package, and Tesla lists the Model 3 package only for 2024 and later Rear-Wheel Drive and All-Wheel Drive cars."),
 ("What is the difference between a tow package and a dealer-installed hitch?",
  "A factory tow package is built into the vehicle on the assembly line and usually bundles more than the receiver: a 7-pin connector, a trailer brake controller, extra cooling and sometimes a higher tow rating. Ford's Maverick 4K Tow Package is the clearest example, since it raises an AWD truck from 2,000 lb to 4,000 lb. A dealer-installed hitch is an accessory bolted on after the build. Ford's 2024 Bronco towing guide lists the Bronco's hitch receiver, harness and trailer module as dealer-installed equipment. An accessory hitch gives you a receiver and sometimes a harness, but it never changes the rating printed in the owner's manual."),
 ("How much tongue weight can a factory receiver carry?",
  "Only what the maker prints, and most makers in this chart print nothing on the pages the site read. Toyota gives 500 lb for the 2010–2024 4Runner. Ford gives 500 lb weight-carrying for the 2024 F-150, 750 lb for the 2024–2026 Ranger (550 lb on the Raptor), 400 lb for the Maverick's 4K package receiver, 350 lb for the Bronco (450 lb on the Raptor) and 560 lb for the 2024 Explorer. Tesla gives 350 lb for the Model Y and 200 lb for the Model 3 with its package. Subaru gives 270 or 350 lb on the Outback by engine. Where the chart says not published, use the owner's manual."),
 ("Does a hitch bike rack count against tongue weight?",
  "Yes, all of it. A platform rack with two e-bikes can weigh well over 100 lb, and every pound of it sits on the receiver as tongue load. The site's Forester guide puts the non-Wilderness tongue limit at 150 lb per a Subaru dealer listing, so a heavy rack alone can reach it. Tesla's manuals give separate carrier limits: 160 lb on the Model Y and 121 lb on the Model 3, and both manuals say not to install an accessory carrier on a car without the Tesla tow package. On larger vehicles the rack rarely hits the limit, but the receiver's own label still applies."),
 ("Why does the chart say not confirmed for the Silverado, Sierra, Ram and Tundra?",
  "Because the site's upgrades pages for those trucks could not confirm, from a Chevrolet, GMC, Ram or Toyota page, that every trim and model year leaves the factory with the receiver fitted. The site's stored data lists a Class IV hitch with a 2 in receiver for each, and the makers publish the maximum tow figures shown in the chart, but a trim-by-trim equipment list was not found. That is not the same as saying the trucks lack a receiver. Most of them have one. It means the page will not print a trim list it did not read, so look under the bumper and read the window sticker."),
 ("Can I tow with a hybrid or EV in this chart?",
  "Within the maker's figures, yes. Toyota rates the Highlander Hybrid at 3,500 lb and the gas 2.4L turbo at 5,000 lb. Honda rates the CR-V hybrid at 1,000 lb against 1,500 lb for gas trims. The RAV4 Hybrid is 1,750 lb and the Prime 2,500 lb in 2024 dealer figures. Ford's 2025 Maverick hybrid with AWD and the 4K package is 4,000 lb; 2022–2024 hybrids are 2,000 lb trucks. Tesla's Model Y is 3,500 lb with its package. The Model 3 is 2,200 lb with trailer brakes on 2024 and later RWD and AWD cars with the package, and Tesla's manual otherwise says not to use the Model 3 for towing."),
 ("Is a 7-pin socket proof of a factory tow package?",
  "It is strong evidence, not proof. On Ford trucks the 7-wire harness and 4-/7-pin connector come with the Trailer Tow Package on the Ranger and Maverick, and Ford's 2024 F-150 guide lists the connector as standard. Chevrolet's 2023 Colorado brochure puts the 7-pin connector in the Trailering Package with the hitch. But a previous owner can add a 7-pin socket with an aftermarket harness, and the site's Telluride guide found a harness listing that says a factory tow package is required, which suggests some Tellurides lack the plug. Treat the socket as one clue, and read the receiver label and window sticker too."),
 ("If my vehicle has a factory receiver, what should I buy?",
  "A ball mount with the right rise or drop, a hitch ball sized to the coupler, a locking pin and, if the trailer has brakes, a brake controller unless the package included one. The site's guides for the Maverick, Explorer and Pilot all say the same thing: a vehicle with a factory receiver does not need an aftermarket trailer hitch, and the hitch makers list their parts for vehicles without one. If a bike rack is the goal, the receiver size decides the rack: 2 in on nearly every vehicle here, 1.25 in on the 2019–2021 Forester accessory hitch and some Model 3 aftermarket parts."),
]

